  "shift_from_previous_round": "In previous rounds, I considered D (Both QJ and QT) based on the 2-card straight symmetry and the assumption of 1-card straight mirror cancellations. However, rigorous combinatorial analysis of Texas Hold'em's 7-card mechanics and the famous poker trivia problem confirms that B (QT) is strictly correct.",
  "why_A_is_wrong": {
    "kicker_heuristic_fallacy": "Against an overpair of pocket Aces, a single pair of Queens, Jacks, or Tens never wins.",
    "two_pair_and_trips_equivalence": "Two pair made with hole cards (Q+J vs. Q+T) both beat AA equally (3x3 combos). On paired boards (e.g. 8-8-Q-J vs 8-8-Q-T), Hero plays AA88Q, rendering J vs T kicker irrelevant. Trips, full houses, and flushes are combinatorially identical between QJ and QT."
  },
  "why_D_is_wrong_and_B_is_correct": {
    "two_card_straights_vs_one_card_straights": "Both QJ and QT make the identical three 2-card straights (A-K-Q-J-T, K-Q-J-T-9, Q-J-T-9-8) due to the Ace cap. However, 1-card straights break the symmetry completely.",
    "the_unmirrored_straight": "Straights are strictly bounded at the top by Ace and do not wrap around. A Jack participates in only 4 straights (all of which also contain a Ten). A Ten participates in 5 straights (including T-9-8-7-6, which contains no Jack and no Queen).",
    "the_flaw_in_the_mirror_argument": "Camp D claims that on T-9-8-7-x, QJ makes a straight while QT pairs the Ten, mirroring 9-8-7-6-x. This is false: the true mirror of T-9-8-7-x for QJ is J-9-8-7-x for QT (where QT makes J-T-9-8-7 and QJ merely pairs the Jack). These two board classes cancel out. That leaves 9-8-7-6-x (where QT makes a T-high straight and QJ has nothing) completely uncounterbalanced, because there is no straight above Broadway for QJ to mirror it.",
    "empirical_solver_equities": "Heads-up against AsAc: QTs (~19.2%) > QJs (~18.9%) > Q9s (~17.5%). Thus, QT relatively minimizes Hero's equity the most."
  },
  "group_dynamics_and_next_steps": {
    "status": "Camp B is gaining momentum with rigorous mathematical proofs from Colleagues 3, 4, and 12. Camp A persists on intuitive/heuristic grounds (Colleagues 1, 5, 7, 9, 13). Camp D (Colleagues 2, 6, 8, 10, 11, 14) is stuck on the 2-card straight symmetry.",
    "strategy_for_next_round": "Directly address Camp D by demonstrating why the alleged symmetry cancellation is an algebraic illusion (pairing J-9-8-7-x with T-9-8-7-x leaves 9-8-7-6-x unmatched), while reminding Camp A that high cards/kickers have 0 value against pocket Aces."
  }
}

