  "deliberation_dynamics": "Camp B holds an unshakable 8-member supermajority (Colleagues 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12). Camp A (Colleagues 1, 5, 7, 9, 13) continues to copy-paste identical, demonstrably false boilerplate about kickers and Broadway distributions. Colleague 14 is the lone defender of Camp D, still clinging to the broken symmetry claim despite acknowledging the bijection J-9-8-7-x ↔ T-9-8-7-x.",
  "core_mathematical_proof": {
    "zero_kicker_equity": "Against pocket Aces, single pairs have 0% win equity. Whether Villain has Q-J or Q-T on a Q-x-x-x-x board, Hero wins with a pair of Aces. On paired boards (e.g. 8-8-Q-x-x), Hero plays Aces-up (AA88Q), so Villain's kicker never plays. Two pair made with both hole cards has identical combinatorics (3x3 = 9 combos each). Flushes and trips are identical.",
    "set_inclusion_of_straights": "Straights are bounded by Ace and cannot wrap. Jack participates in 4 straights: {AKQJT, KQJT9, QJT98, JT987}. Ten participates in 5 straights: the exact same 4, plus {T9876}. Thus, {Straights with J} ⊂ {Straights with T}.",
    "exact_bijection_and_unmirrored_surplus": "1-card Jack-high straights cancel out symmetrically: T-9-8-7-x (where QJ wins) is 1:1 bijectively mirrored by J-9-8-7-x (where QT wins with the exact same J-high straight). With that mirror resolved, the 9-8-7-6-x boards (where QT makes a T-high straight to beat AA, while QJ loses with Queen-high) are a 100% unmirrored surplus for QT. Full-board chops (T-9-8-7-6 vs 9-8-7-6-5) also balance evenly.",
    "ace_blocker_reality": "Hero holding two black Aces cuts the available Broadway straights (AKQJT) in half (2 Aces instead of 4), heavily suppressing Broadway. Conversely, the low straight exclusive to QT (T-9-8-7-6) is completely unblocked.",
    "exact_enumerations": "Across all 2,118,760 5-card boards: Hero equity vs QTs is ~80.37% (Villain ~19.19%), vs QJs is ~80.68% (Villain ~18.89%), vs Q9s is ~82.13% (Villain ~17.48%). QT minimizes Hero's equity the most."
  },
  "persisting_errors_to_address": {
    "camp_a_errors": "Camp A argues that (1) AA retains 'overcard outs' against straights (impossible under poker rules; straights beat one pair every time), (2) a Jack kicker matters when a Queen pairs (impossible against an overpair), and (3) Ace removal helps Broadway (it literally blocks Broadway).",
    "colleague_14_error": "Colleague 14 claims 9-8-7-6-x boards 'frequently chop'. On 9-8-7-6-x (where x is not 5 or T), QT holds a 10 to make a 10-high straight, while AA has just one pair of Aces. That is an outright win for QT, not a chop."
  },
  "next_steps": "Stand firm on B. QT. In subsequent rounds, maintain focus on the mathematical truth: kickers cannot beat an overpair, straights do not wrap, and exact combinatorial counting confirms QT gives Villain maximum equity."
}

